Let the Ogg Vorbis writer and reader carry LOOPSTART / LOOPLENGTH

Game engines that loop background music from the file itself (RPG Maker
MV/MZ and many others) read the loop from two Vorbis comments: LOOPSTART,
the first sample of the loop, and LOOPLENGTH, its length in samples. JUCE's
Ogg writer only writes a fixed list of comments -- the ID3-like ones -- and
drops every other metadata key, so there was no way to write them. This adds
the two to both lists, under their own names as metadata keys: the writer
writes them when the metadata has them, and the reader reports them, so a
render can be checked by reading it back.

